# Credentials known to have appeared in this repository, by SHA-256 of the
# literal value. The VALUES are never written here — that would defeat the file.
#
# FORMAT:  <sha256>  <max-occurrences>  <name>  # note
#
# `max-occurrences` is a RATCHET, not a target. A count that grows fails the
# check; a count that shrinks is a fix, and the number is lowered in the same
# commit that shrinks it. Zero means the literal must never appear.
#
# Compute a candidate's hash without writing it down:
#   printf '%s' "$SECRET" | sha256sum

44acccf4a551b57fac8a0aed1fb56bbfac849abefeae735afb199f1704dec48e  0  ODDS_API_KEY (exhausted free tier)
# Removed 2026-08-25 from src/index.js:572, src/wp-resolver.js:42,
# .github/workflows/odds-backfill.yml, and outbox/cc-odds-audit-results.md;
# .github/workflows/update-odds-key.yml was DELETED rather than edited — its
# only job was `wrangler secret put ODDS_API_KEY` from this literal, under a
# step named "Set ODDS_API_KEY to 20K plan key" that echoed
# "✅ ODDS_API_KEY updated to 20K plan key". One workflow_dispatch would have
# replaced the working production key with this one and reported success. A
# workflow whose purpose is to write a secret from a repo literal cannot be
# fixed by editing it; the correct value is not in this repo and must not be.
# Zero, because nothing needs it: GET /budget/odds on 2026-08-25 showed 42,102
# credits used this month against an 85,000 limit, so env.ODDS_API_KEY is set
# and working and this constant was never reached.

06e2d4f3752f87d37dc44d4796203edd94b3a5818a0bd3bdb719587ad764643b  0  ODDS_API_KEY_FALLBACK (Starter plan, 500 credits/month)
# THE THIRD ODDS CREDENTIAL, and the one no ratchet could see until 2026-10-12.
#
# There are three. The free-tier key above was removed from source 2026-08-25
# and pinned at 0. The live 20K-plan key was rotated by the owner 2026-09-15 and
# was never a literal in any repo. This one is neither: it is the Starter-tier
# key that env.ODDS_API_KEY_FALLBACK holds, it was never rotated, and it sat in
# plaintext in six tracked locations across two repos the whole time.
#
# It was invisible for a structural reason worth stating: a ratchet only knows
# the hashes it is given. check-exposed-secrets.mjs reported PASS at
# "ODDS_API_KEY (exhausted free tier): at most 0 occurrence(s)" every single run
# while six copies of a DIFFERENT odds key sat in docs/ and outbox/. A green
# scanner said nothing about this value because nobody had ever written this
# line. Measured 2026-10-12 by hashing every 32-hex token in all four repos:
#   field-relay-nba   1  docs/CC-CMD-2026-06-16-odds-quota-audit.md
#   jubilant-bassoon  5  .coderabbit.yaml x1, docs/CC-CMD-2026-06-16-odds-quota-audit.md x1,
#                        outbox/cc-odds-audit-results.md x3
#   field-laboratory  0
#   field-playground  0
#
# The 2026-08-25 redaction pass walked past it. In
# jubilant-bassoon/outbox/cc-odds-audit-results.md it replaced the free-tier key
# with "<redacted 2026-08-25 ...>" on lines ADJACENT to this one and left this
# one standing — because that pass was chasing one known value, not scanning for
# credential-shaped tokens.
#
# And jubilant-bassoon/.coderabbit.yaml:26 is a review instruction that reads
# "never hardcode the Starter key (<the key>)". The same joke as the secret
# guard that shipped with the real key in its own test fixture (commit 26c2507).
#
# ZERO, and the literals are gone as of 2026-10-12.
#
# AND THE VALUE IS DEAD: the owner rotated it at the provider on 2026-10-12.
# That is what ended it. The literals in git history are still readable — in
# both repos, history was not rewritten — but what they read out is no longer a
# credential. Redaction stopped the spread; rotation is what mattered.
#
# THE RATCHET STAYS AT ZERO ANYWAY. A dead key pasted into a document is how
# the free-tier entry above began, and that one is still being chased through
# four repos' history two months later. Zero is about the habit, not the value.
#
# UNVERIFIED, deliberately recorded as such: nothing in this repo probes
# ODDS_API_KEY_FALLBACK. See docs/CC-CMD-2026-10-12-starter-key-exposure.md.

c3118a4a99dfe805e724cc1da7bba35635e0c09341bf1a25ca9a6fb01e614638  86  RELAY_SHARED_SECRET (cron/self-call shared secret)
# NOT zero, and deliberately so.
#
# THE COUNT WAS WORSE THAN RECORDED, TWICE. First it was described as "27 in
# src/index.js, 1 in analytics-engine.js, 5 workflows, 8 docs" -- a figure from
# grepping src/. This scanner's first run measured 114 across 70 files. Then the
# scanner ITSELF was found to have a hole (a secret inside a nested quote span
# was invisible -- see scripts/check-exposed-secrets.mjs) and the corrected
# count is 115. Each number was reported as the exposure and each was low:
#
#   src/index.js                    27
#
# LOWERED 115 -> 109 on 2026-09-20. src/index.js had EIGHT copies of this literal
# as an inbound route gate (`authHeader !== '...'`), one per CI-only route. They
# are now one named constant, CI_ROUTE_GATE, so src/index.js holds 20 rather than
# 27 and a ninth CI route costs no ninth copy.
#
# The ratchet is why this happened at all: /debug/odds-budget-latency added a
# 116th and the gate went red on the commit that did it. Adding one to the
# number would have been the quick route; naming the literal once was the
# correct one, and it ends with a TIGHTER bound than before rather than a
# looser one. The remaining 20 in that file send the header on OUTBOUND calls
# rather than checking it, which is a different shape and a separate change.
#   ~40 scripts/*.mjs                1 each
#   6 workflows                      1-2 each
#   ~20 docs/ and outbox/ files      1-6 each
#   src/analytics-engine.js          1
#   workers/field-claude-proxy       1
#
# AND THIS REPO IS NOT THE ONLY ONE. A cross-repo scan on 2026-08-25 found
# jubilant-bassoon carrying 9 more, four of them live code, plus three
# occurrences of the Odds API key that had just been declared removed. See
# jubilant-bassoon docs/exposed-secrets.sha256. field-laboratory and
# field-playground measured clean.
#
# The scripts matter most and were the part nobody had counted. Each one reaches
# a credentialled write path -- POST /d1/execute among them -- with the secret
# compiled in rather than read from the environment, so the credential travels
# with a clone of this repository.
#
# ELEVEN of them are inbound AUTH GATES, not one: 3997 (the only `===` form),
# 13496, 13944, 14734, 14758, 14778, 14807, 14833, 15012, 15076 -- and 3997
# gates POST /d1/execute. Parsed from HEAD 2026-08-25; an earlier note here
# named 3997 alone.
#
# Removing the literals WITHOUT rotating first buys nothing — the value is in
# git history either way — and risks real breakage: if the env binding is unset,
# the auth comparison becomes `header === undefined`, and every relay self-call
# starts sending an empty secret. That is a coordinated change and it has an
# order, and it is NOT rotate-first: .github/workflows/bootstrap-relay-secret.yml
# extracts this value FROM src/index.js, so rotating before the source is clean
# leaves a dispatchable job that reinstalls the old one -- the same shape as the
# deleted update-odds-key.yml. Source first (a no-op at runtime while the value
# is unchanged, so it can be verified live), then retire bootstrap, then rotate,
# then lower this number to 0. The ~40 scripts must move to
# `process.env.RELAY_SHARED_SECRET` with NO default, the way
# field-laboratory's sport-vocabulary-check.mjs already does -- a default makes
# an unset secret indistinguishable from a set one until the relay 401s.
#
# See docs/CC-CMD-2026-08-25-rotate-relay-secret.md. Until then this line is a
# ratchet: the count may not grow, so a new hard-coded use fails the deploy.
